  "finding": "At the shipped 0.92 bar all character/token recall is 0 \u2014 but rank@1 is 88-96%, so the correct decision is the top match; the failure is the BAR, not the matcher. Swept, StringMatcher's clean knee is bar 0.45 -> 75% recall at 0 wrong-key; TokenOverlap ~62% at 0.45. Calibrating the bar (nestor calibrate) recovers most re-wordings with no false constraints on this corpus. Below ~0.40 the wrong-key (false-constraint) cost begins (the S1.1 near-duplicate inversion), and on a corpus of genuine near-duplicate decisions the clean window shrinks. The residual ~25% that score under the knee even for the correct decision are the tail a semantic matcher would address. The semantic stand-in's 100%/0 remains an optimistic upper bound (author=scorer, recognition not graded similarity)."
